        "---\n",
        "title: \"Neural Networks for Text\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Embeddings, softmax classifiers, and trainable nonlinear maps\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Classical text models often use a fixed representation such as a count vector, TF--IDF vector, or SVD coordinate.  A **neural network for text** replaces part of this representation pipeline by a trainable map.  Tokens become vectors, vectors are combined into a document or sequence representation, and the resulting representation is sent through nonlinear maps and a softmax output layer.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal of this chapter is not to treat neural networks as black boxes.  The goal is to write down the maps, dimensions, losses, and gradients clearly enough that the model becomes a familiar object from linear algebra, probability, and optimization.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. describe a neural text classifier as a composition of maps;\n",
        "2. distinguish fixed text features from trainable embeddings;\n",
        "3. compute softmax probabilities and cross-entropy loss;\n",
        "4. derive the gradient of softmax cross-entropy with respect to logits;\n",
        "5. explain how an embedding layer receives gradients from a sentence-level loss;\n",
        "6. implement a small neural text classifier from scratch in Python;\n",
        "7. interpret regularization, temperature, and calibration for neural text models.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.1 The modeling pipeline\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple neural text classifier has four conceptual steps.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{text}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{token indices}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{token embeddings}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{text representation}\n",
        "\\longrightarrow\n",
        "\\text{class probabilities}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $V = \\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ be a finite vocabulary.  A document or sentence is represented as a token sequence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_T) \\in V^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A neural model is a parameterized function\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_\\theta: V^T \\to \\Delta^{K-1},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $K$ is the number of classes and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Delta^{K-1}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\left\\{p \\in \\mathbb R^K: p_k \\ge 0,\\ \\sum_{k=1}^K p_k=1\\right\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is the probability simplex.  The output $f_\\theta(x)$ is interpreted as a conditional distribution over labels:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_\\theta(x)_k \\approx P(Y=k\\mid X=x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A minimal neural text classifier can be written as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "e_i &= E_{x_i} \\in \\mathbb R^m, \\qquad i=1,\\ldots,T,\\\\\n",
        "h(x) &= \\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{i=1}^T e_i \\in \\mathbb R^m,\\\\\n",
        "r(x) &= \\sigma(Ah(x)+a) \\in \\mathbb R^q,\\\\\n",
        "z(x) &= Wr(x)+b \\in \\mathbb R^K,\\\\\n",
        "f_\\theta(x) &= \\operatorname{softmax}(z(x)).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $E\\in \\mathbb R^{d\\times m}$ is an **embedding matrix**;\n",
        "- $E_j$ is the row vector representing token $j$;\n",
        "- $h(x)$ is an averaged sentence representation;\n",
        "- $A\\in \\mathbb R^{q\\times m}$ and $a\\in \\mathbb R^q$ define a hidden layer;\n",
        "- $\\sigma$ is a nonlinear activation such as ReLU or tanh;\n",
        "- $W\\in \\mathbb R^{K\\times q}$ and $b\\in \\mathbb R^K$ define the output logits;\n",
        "- $\\theta=(E,A,a,W,b)$ denotes all trainable parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Core idea.**  A neural text classifier is a composition of a discrete lookup map, vector averaging or sequence aggregation, nonlinear transformations, and a probabilistic output map.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.2 From fixed features to trainable features\n",
        "\n",
        "Earlier chapters studied fixed representation maps such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi(x) \\in \\mathbb R^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\phi(x)$ may be a count vector, a TF--IDF vector, or an SVD coordinate.  A linear classifier then uses\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z(x)=W\\phi(x)+b.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In a neural model, part of $\\phi$ is learned from data.  For example, an embedding layer defines\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi_E(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{i=1}^T E_{x_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The representation depends on trainable parameters $E$.  Therefore training changes not only the classifier weights, but also the geometry in which tokens are represented.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the main mathematical shift:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{fixed representation } \\phi\n",
        "\\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad\n",
        "\\text{trainable representation } \\phi_\\theta.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The statistical question also changes.  We are no longer only estimating class probabilities after selecting features.  We are jointly estimating a feature map and a classifier.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.3 The embedding layer as a matrix lookup\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $E\\in \\mathbb R^{d\\times m}$.  If token $j\\in V$ is represented by the one-hot vector $u_j\\in \\mathbb R^d$, then its embedding is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "E^T u_j \\in \\mathbb R^m.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently, if $E_j$ denotes the $j$th row of $E$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "E^T u_j = E_j^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus an embedding layer is just a matrix multiplication by a one-hot vector, implemented efficiently as a row lookup.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_T)$, the embedding layer produces a matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "M(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "E_{x_1}\\\\\n",
        "E_{x_2}\\\\\n",
        "\\vdots\\\\\n",
        "E_{x_T}\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in \\mathbb R^{T\\times m}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple sentence representation is average pooling:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h(x)=\\frac{1}{T}\\mathbf 1_T^T M(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This representation is permutation-invariant.  It ignores word order.  That is a serious limitation for some language tasks, but it is a useful first model because its mathematics is transparent.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Dimension check.**  If $E\\in \\mathbb R^{d\\times m}$ and $M(x)\\in \\mathbb R^{T\\times m}$, then average pooling gives $h(x)\\in \\mathbb R^m$.  If the hidden layer has width $q$ and there are $K$ classes, then $A\\in \\mathbb R^{q\\times m}$ and $W\\in \\mathbb R^{K\\times q}$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.4 Activations and nonlinear maps\n",
        "\n",
        "A neural network becomes more than a linear classifier because of nonlinear activation functions.  Common choices include\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{ReLU}(t)=\\max\\{0,t\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\tanh(t)=\\frac{e^t-e^{-t}}{e^t+e^{-t}},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the logistic sigmoid\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sigma(t)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-t}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Applied coordinatewise, an activation maps vectors to vectors:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sigma(v)=(\\sigma(v_1),\\ldots,\\sigma(v_q)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A one-hidden-layer classifier is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z(x)=W\\sigma(Ah(x)+a)+b.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Without the activation, this collapses to another affine map:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W(Ah+a)+b=(WA)h+(Wa+b).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore the activation is what allows the model to represent nonlinear decision boundaries in the representation space.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.5 Softmax output and class probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "For $K$ classes, the model produces logits\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_K)\\in \\mathbb R^K.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The softmax function maps logits to probabilities:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}(z)_k\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{e^{z_k}}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^K e^{z_\\ell}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Softmax is invariant under adding a constant to all logits:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}(z+c\\mathbf 1)=\\operatorname{softmax}(z).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This identity is important for numerical stability.  In computation, we usually evaluate\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}(z)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}(z-\\max_j z_j\\mathbf 1).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For binary classification with classes $0$ and $1$, the two-class softmax is equivalent to logistic regression.  If\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_1-z_0=s,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid x)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-s}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus a neural binary classifier can be interpreted as logistic regression applied after a learned nonlinear representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.6 Cross-entropy and maximum likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the training data are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal D_n=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\}_{i=1}^n,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $y_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$.  Let $p_\\theta(x_i)=f_\\theta(x_i)$.  The model assigns probability\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(y_i\\mid x_i)=p_\\theta(x_i)_{y_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The conditional likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{i=1}^n p_\\theta(y_i\\mid x_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Maximizing likelihood is equivalent to minimizing negative log-likelihood:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n \\log p_\\theta(y_i\\mid x_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $e_{y_i}\\in\\mathbb R^K$ is the one-hot vector for the true label, the per-example loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell_i(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\sum_{k=1}^K (e_{y_i})_k \\log p_\\theta(x_i)_k.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the cross-entropy between the empirical one-hot label distribution and the model's predicted distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "For empirical risk minimization, we usually minimize the average loss:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R_n(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n \\ell_i(\\theta).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.7 The key gradient: softmax plus cross-entropy\n",
        "\n",
        "The softmax cross-entropy gradient has a remarkably simple form.  Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p=\\operatorname{softmax}(z),\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\ell(z,y)=-\\log p_y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial \\ell}{\\partial z_k}=p_k-\\mathbf 1\\{k=y\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In vector notation,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla_z \\ell=p-e_y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula is one reason softmax cross-entropy is convenient for classification.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Derivation\n",
        "\n",
        "First note that\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log p_y\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{e^{z_y}}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^K e^{z_\\ell}}\\right)\n",
        "=\n",
        "z_y-\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\sum_{\\ell=1}^K e^{z_\\ell}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(z,y)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-z_y+\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\sum_{\\ell=1}^K e^{z_\\ell}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Differentiating with respect to $z_k$ gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial \\ell}{\\partial z_k}\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\mathbf 1\\{k=y\\}\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\frac{e^{z_k}}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^K e^{z_\\ell}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "p_k-\\mathbf 1\\{k=y\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Statistical interpretation.**  The vector $p-e_y$ is the difference between the predicted class distribution and the empirical one-hot class distribution.  Gradient descent pushes predicted probabilities toward the observed labels.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.8 Backpropagation through an averaged embedding model\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the simple model\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{i=1}^T E_{x_i},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "z=Wh+b,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "p=\\operatorname{softmax}(z),\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\ell=-\\log p_y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "g_z=\\nabla_z\\ell=p-e_y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then the gradients are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla_W\\ell=g_z h^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla_b\\ell=g_z,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "g_h=\\nabla_h\\ell=W^Tg_z.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{i=1}^T E_{x_i},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "each token embedding in the document receives the gradient\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\nabla_{E_{x_i}}\\ell = \\frac{1}{T}g_h.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If the same token appears multiple times, its row in $E$ accumulates multiple contributions.  This is why embedding layers are trainable even though token indices are discrete: the gradient flows into the continuous row vectors selected by the indices.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.9 Optimization and regularization\n",
        "\n",
        "Neural text models are usually trained with stochastic optimization.  For a mini-batch $B\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R_B(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{|B|}\\sum_{i\\in B}\\ell_i(\\theta).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A stochastic gradient descent update is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{t+1}=\\theta_t-\\eta_t\\nabla_\\theta \\widehat R_B(\\theta_t),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\eta_t>0$ is a learning rate.\n",
        "\n",
        "A regularized objective may be written as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "J(\\theta)=\\widehat R_n(\\theta)+\\lambda\\|\\theta\\|_2^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In practice, one often regularizes only selected parameters, such as $A$ and $W$, not necessarily the embedding matrix.  Dropout, early stopping, and weight decay are also commonly used.\n",
        "\n",
        "From a statistical perspective, regularization is important because neural models often have many more parameters than training examples.  Without regularization or inductive bias, empirical loss alone may not control generalization.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.10 Temperature, confidence, and calibration\n",
        "\n",
        "For logits $z$, define temperature-scaled softmax by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_k(\\tau)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\exp(z_k/\\tau)}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^K\\exp(z_\\ell/\\tau)},\n",
        "\\qquad \\tau>0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Small $\\tau$ makes the distribution sharper.  Large $\\tau$ makes it flatter.  Temperature does not change the ranking of logits, but it changes the confidence assigned to predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "This distinction matters in NLP because a model can be accurate but poorly calibrated.  Calibration asks whether predicted probabilities match empirical frequencies.  For example, among all examples to which a model assigns confidence near $0.8$, approximately $80\\%$ should be correct if the model is calibrated.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 10.11 Interactive: Softmax classifier and temperature\n",
        "\n",
        "The following interactive illustrates a binary softmax classifier in a two-feature representation space.  Think of the horizontal coordinate as the strength of evidence for technical or mathematical language and the vertical coordinate as the strength of evidence for casual or nontechnical language.  The sliders control the logit difference\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s(x)=w_1x_1+w_2x_2+b,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the model probability\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid x)=\\frac{1}{1+\\exp(-s(x)/\\tau)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
